 3, \"type\": \"table\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [68, 1816, 352, 1856], \"page_id\": 64}], \"content\": \"Source: Reports of collections. Less than $500.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [691, 53, 721, 80], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"51\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [198, 131, 1204, 197], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"Study of Individual Income Tax Returns Concerning Differences Between State of Residence and State of Filing\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 1, \"type\": \"title\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [108, 237, 1300, 499], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"A recent study of all Federal individual income tax returns for 1936 shows that while ordinarily a taxpayer's place of residence and his filing district are located in the same State, some taxpayers living in one State file their income tax returns in another State. The present tabulation represents part of an extensive study of Federal tax returns recently completed under the sponsorship of the Treasury Department, Division of Tax Research, with funds allocated to the Department by the Commissioner of Work Projects under authorization of the President. It supplements material published in \\\"Statistics of Income for 1936, Part 1,\\\" and in \\\"Statistics of Income Supplement Compiled from Income Tax Returns for 1936\\\".\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 2, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [106, 526, 1300, 869], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"The Revenue Act of 1936 and accompanying Regulations governing the filing of the 1936 returns provided that a return should be filed in the district of the taxpayer's legal residence or that of the principal place of his own business, and that returns of individuals with no legal residence or place of business in the United States should be filed at Baltimore, Maryland. (Revenue Act of 1936, Section 53 (b)(1), and Regulations 94, Article 53-5.) A number of taxpayers who obviously failed to follow the filing requirements were mainly salaried workers and wage earners who filed in the district in which they were employed, and taxpayers required to file in Baltimore who erroneously filed in other districts. In a few cases it was not possible to determine the residence of a taxpayer since residential address was not given as required when the return was filed at the business address. Returns that gave no address, numbering less than 1,600, were excluded from the study.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 3, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [105, 898, 1304, 1249], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"Of the total of 5,367,580 individual returns tabulated, including 57,429 returns of estates and trusts filed by fiduciaries, 5,191,223 returns showed residence of taxpayers to be within State of filing. The remaining 176,357 returns, or 3.3 percent of the total, reported residence outside State of filing. (See Table I.) However, included in the latter total are the returns of 104,497 residents of the District of Columbia and 4,554 \\\"foreign\\\" residents who filed in Maryland, and 4,350 returns of residents of Alaska who filed in Washington according to filing requirements. (See Tables III and IV.) Excluding this group of returns, the number of returns filed outside State of filing is reduced to 62,956, or 1.2 percent of all returns tabulated. Explanation of the minor variations existing between the two classifications for these returns can be found to a large extent in the proximity of State of residence to that of filing, notably in the case of New Jersey and Connecticut residents who filed in New York.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 4, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [106, 1275, 1305, 1422], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"As in the case of number of returns, the amount of statutory net income 1/ reported on returns showing residence outside of State of filing represented only a small proportion (3.4 percent) of all such income reported. Comparison by individual States of the income reported on a residence basis as against a filing basis of classification shows essentially the same relationship as is shown for number of returns. (See Table II.) 2/\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 5, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [106, 1450, 1309, 1648], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"The practice of classifying individual income tax returns on the basis of the filing districts of the taxpayers has long been followed in compiling State data in \\\"Statistics of Income\\\" and other official statistics. The data presented here show that classification of returns on the basis of reported residence does not result in substantial differences between the two methods. The items reported by nonresidents in each State offset to a considerable extent the items reported on returns of residents filing in other States.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 6, \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [106, 1663, 1302, 1732], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"1/ Statutory net income (or deficit) represents the amount of gross income reported on Forms 1040 and 1040A minus deductions claimed by the taxpayer. Credits against net income allowed for tax computation are not included in deductions.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 7, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [106, 1732, 1297, 1796], \"page_id\": 65}], \"content\": \"2/ Tables similar to Tables I and II for the items total income, total deductions, and salaries and wages, are available in source books on file with the Treasury Department. Also available is a more detailed table from which Tables III and IV were derived and a similar table relating to statutory net income.\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 8, \"type\": \"footnote\"}, {\"bbox\": [{\"coord\": [716, 38, 756, 60], \"page_id\": 66}], \"content\": \"52\", \"description\": null, \"id\": 0, \"type\": \"page_number\"}, {\